# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AlphaEngine
#
# Every pattern here is ANCHORED (leading `/`) or extension-scoped, on purpose.
# A bare directory name like `lib/` or `build/` matches at ANY depth, so pasting
# in a language template silently swallowed `Part2_Infrastructure/web/lib/` — new source files there
# were skipped by `git add -A` without a word and the deployment failed with
# "Module not found: Can't resolve '@/lib/livebook'".
#
# Keep patterns anchored so one language's build-output rule cannot reach into
# another's source tree. `Part2_Infrastructure/tools/check_repo_complete.sh` guards this.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── secrets — never commit ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ── OS / editor / docs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.pdf

# ── Python (scoped to the gateway) ───────────────────────────────────────────
/Part2_Infrastructure/venv/
/Part2_Infrastructure/.venv/
/Part2_Infrastructure/build/
/Part2_Infrastructure/dist/
/Part2_Infrastructure/.eggs/
/Part2_Infrastructure/.tox/
/Part2_Infrastructure/htmlcov/
/Part2_Infrastructure/.coverage
*.egg-info/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# ── Runtime data (recreated on startup) ──────────────────────────────────────
/Part2_Infrastructure/data/*
# Re-include the placeholder so the directory survives a clone. This only works
# because the pattern above excludes the directory's *contents* (`/*`) rather
# than the directory itself — git will not descend into an excluded directory,
# so `data/` would have made this negation dead.
!/Part2_Infrastructure/data/.gitkeep
*.duckdb
*.duckdb.wal
*.sqlite

# ── Vercel link at the repo root ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# The web project's Root Directory is `Part2_Infrastructure/web`, so the CLI has
# to be run from *here* — deploying from inside `web/` makes Vercel append the
# root directory a second time and look for `web/Part2_Infrastructure/web`. That
# means a `.vercel/` link lands at the repo root, and it carries project and org
# ids that have no business in the history.
/.vercel/

# ── Node / Next.js (scoped to the portal) ────────────────────────────────────
/Part2_Infrastructure/web/node_modules/
/Part2_Infrastructure/web/.next/
/Part2_Infrastructure/web/out/
/Part2_Infrastructure/web/.vercel/
/Part2_Infrastructure/web/next-env.d.ts
/Part2_Infrastructure/web/*.tsbuildinfo
/Part2_Infrastructure/web/.turbo/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# `vercel link` appends `.vercel` and `.env*` here every time it runs. Both are
# already covered above — `/.vercel/` and the `.env` / `.env.*` pair — and the
# generated `.env*` is worse than redundant: it sits below `!.env.example` and
# so silently re-ignores it, which would drop any *new* `.env.example` from the
# repo exactly the way the note at the top of this file describes. Delete those
# two lines again after re-linking.
